{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qhy7wnYaZGuCdjE418sQBL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Multi-stream GKR Input Evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce LogUp GKR time at APC 300 by parallelizing the GKR input evaluation across multiple OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams. This targets the single largest component of STARK excl trace (808ms / 41%), specifically the `log_gkr_input_evals()` function which launches 791 sequential per-AIR kernel calls totaling 428ms of GPU time. With 4 concurrent streams, we expect to reduce input eval wall clock from ~450ms to ~180-220ms, yielding a ~200-250ms reduction in LogUp GKR and a ~10-13% improvement in STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:274` calls `log_gkr_input_evals()` at line 344.\n12\t\n13\t### Target function\n14\t\n15\t`log_gkr_input_evals()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:89-221`:\n16\t\n17\t1. **Line 102-103**: Allocates `leaves` buffer (`DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` with capacity `total_leaves`) and calls `fill_zero()`.\n18\t2. **Lines 106-107**: Creates shared mutable buffers `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` (reused across iterations).\n19\t3. **Lines 108-216**: Sequential `for meta in trace_interactions.iter().flatten()` loop. For each AIR with interactions:\n20\t   - **Lines 112-121**: Gathers preprocessed matrix and partitioned main matrices (read-only pointers).\n21\t   - **Lines 130-133**: H2D copy of `public_values` (small, per-AIR).\n22\t   - **Lines 138-145**: H2D copy of `partition_ptrs` into `d_partition_ptrs` (reused buffer, small).\n23\t   - **Lines 147-154**: Allocates `intermediates` buffer (per-iteration, freed at loop end).\n24\t   - **Lines 158-176**: Computes `dst_offset` into `leaves`, determines if lifting is needed, gets `trace_output` pointer (either `leaves_ptr` or `tmp`).\n25\t   - **Lines 177-191**: Launches `logup_gkr_input_eval` GPU kernel.\n26\t   - **Lines 193-214**: If lifting needed, launches `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` and `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` GPU kernels.\n27\t\n28\t### Why it is slow\n29\t\n30\tNsight profiling at APC 300 shows:\n31\t- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>`: 374.9ms across 486 instances (avg 771us each)\n32\t- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`: 53.6ms across 305 instances (avg 176us each)\n33\t- Total: 428.5ms GPU kernel time, ~450ms wall clock\n34\t\n35\tEach kernel uses 1-4 SMs on the RTX 4090's 128 SMs. Running sequentially on a single CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`), >95% of GPU compute capacity is idle during each kernel launch. The per-AIR operations are fully independent — they read from different trace partitions and write to non-overlapping offsets in the `leaves` buffer.\n36\t\n37\t### Successful precedent\n38\t\n39\tThe Round 0 multi-stream optimization (task `2026-04-13-0100-multistream-round0-parallel`) achieved 1.89x improvement at APC 300 using the same pattern: distributing independent per-AIR work across 4 OS threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread`. The kernel size distribution is similar (Round 0 avg ~600us, GKR input avg ~540us).\n40\t\n41\t## Changes\n42\t\n43\t### Change 1: Add `GkrInputWorkItem` struct\n44\t\n45\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n46\t\n47\tAdd a struct that holds all read-only references needed to process one AIR's GKR input evaluation:\n48\t\n49\t```rust\n50\tstruct GkrInputWorkItem<'a, HS: GpuHashScheme> {\n51\t    meta: &'a TraceInteractionMeta,\n52\t    air_ctx: &'a AirProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n53\t    pk_air: &'a DeviceStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n54\t    l_skip: usize,\n55\t    d_challenges: &'a DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n56\t    /// Raw pointer into the pre-allocated, zero-filled `leaves` buffer at this AIR's offset.\n57\t    leaves_ptr: *mut Frac<EF>,\n58\t}\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\t**Why**: Encapsulates per-AIR data so that the worker function can process one AIR independently, without mutable shared state. The `leaves_ptr` is a raw pointer because different AIRs write to non-overlapping regions of the same buffer; Rust's borrow checker cannot express this directly, but it is safe because the stacked layout guarantees non-overlapping offsets.\n62\t\n63\t`leaves_ptr` must be wrapped in a newtype that implements `Send`:\n64\t\n65\t```rust\n66\tstruct SendPtr<T>(*mut T);\n67\tunsafe impl<T> Send for SendPtr<T> {}\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t### Change 2: Add `process_gkr_input_air()` function\n71\t\n72\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n73\t\n74\tExtract the loop body (lines 109-215) into a standalone function:\n75\t\n76\t```rust\n77\tfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n78\t    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n79\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n80\t    // ... same logic as the current loop body, but using w.leaves_ptr\n81\t    // instead of indexing into a shared leaves buffer.\n82\t    // Each call creates its own:\n83\t    //   - d_partition_ptrs (H2D copy)\n84\t    //   - intermediates (GPU buffer)\n85\t    //   - tmp (GPU buffer, if lifting needed)\n86\t    //   - d_public_values (H2D copy)\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t**Why**: A standalone function can be called from any thread. Each thread will get its own `cudaStreamPerThread`, so GPU kernel launches from different threads execute on different CUDA streams concurrently.\n91\t\n92\tPer-thread buffer allocation is fine here: `d_partition_ptrs` is tiny (2-3 pointers = 16-24 bytes), `intermediates` capacity is at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` (typically a few MB), and `tmp` is `height * num_interactions` (varies but bounded by the largest AIR). The original code already allocated `intermediates` per-iteration, so peak memory usage per-thread is similar to the original sequential code.\n93\t\n94\t### Change 3: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals()` into 3 phases\n95\t\n96\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n97\t\n98\tReplace the sequential loop with a three-phase structure:\n99\t\n100\t**Phase 1 (CPU, main thread)**: Build work items and sort by descending height for load balance.\n101\t\n102\t```rust\n103\tlet mut work_items: Vec<GkrInputWorkItem<HS>> = trace_interactions\n104\t    .iter()\n105\t    .flatten()\n106\t    .map(|meta| {\n107\t        let air_ctx = &ctx.per_trace[meta.trace_idx].1;\n108\t        let pk_air = &pk.per_air[meta.air_idx];\n109\t        let slice = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap();\n110\t        assert_eq!(slice.col_idx, 0);\n111\t        let dst_offset = slice.row_idx;\n112\t        let leaves_ptr = unsafe { leaves.as_mut_ptr().add(dst_offset) };\n113\t        GkrInputWorkItem {\n114\t            meta,\n115\t            air_ctx,\n116\t            pk_air,\n117\t            l_skip,\n118\t            d_challenges,\n119\t            leaves_ptr: SendPtr(leaves_ptr),\n120\t        }\n121\t    })\n122\t    .collect();\n123\t\n124\twork_items.sort_by(|a, b| b.air_ctx.height().cmp(&a.air_ctx.height()));\n125\t```\n126\t\n127\t**Sync barrier**: Call `current_stream_sync()` to ensure the `leaves.fill_zero()` and any prior GPU work (trace uploads, pk data) is visible to worker thread streams.\n128\t\n129\t**Phase 2 (GPU, multi-threaded)**: Process AIRs in parallel.\n130\t\n131\t```rust\n132\tconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;\n133\t\n134\tlet num_threads = if work_items.len() >= 100 {\n135\t    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(work_items.len())\n136\t} else {\n137\t    1\n138\t};\n139\t\n140\tif num_threads <= 1 {\n141\t    for w in &work_items {\n142\t        process_gkr_input_air(w)?;\n143\t    }\n144\t} else {\n145\t    let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n146\t    std::thread::scope(|s| {\n147\t        let handles: Vec<_> = work_items\n148\t            .chunks(chunk_size)\n149\t            .map(|chunk| {\n150\t                s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n151\t                    for w in chunk {\n152\t                        process_gkr_input_air(w)?;\n153\t                    }\n154\t                    Ok(())\n155\t                })\n156\t            })\n157\t            .collect();\n158\t        for handle in handles {\n159\t            handle.join().unwrap()?;\n160\t        }\n161\t        Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n162\t    })?;\n163\t}\n164\t```\n165\t\n166\t**Why**: Same approach as the successful Round 0 multi-stream. `std::thread::scope` with N threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread` for concurrent kernel execution. The threshold of 100 AIRs avoids multi-threading at APC 0 (99 AIRs), preventing the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task.\n167\t\n168\t**Phase 3**: None needed. Unlike Round 0, the results are written directly into the `leaves` buffer via raw pointers during Phase 2 — there is no CPU-side scatter step.\n169\t\n170\t### Change 4: Reuse `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` constant or define `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS`\n171\t\n172\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n173\t\n174\tDefine a constant `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;` for the number of worker threads. This matches `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` in `mod.rs:93` and can be tuned independently if needed.\n175\t\n176\t**Why**: Keeps the GKR input parallelism configuration independent from Round 0, in case they need different tuning (e.g., if GKR input has different memory pressure characteristics).\n177\t\n178\t## Invariants\n179\t\n180\t1. **Correctness**: Each AIR writes to a non-overlapping region of `leaves`, determined by `dst_offset = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap().row_idx`. The stacked layout guarantees these regions do not overlap. The `fill_zero()` call happens before the sync barrier, ensuring all regions start zeroed.\n181\t\n182\t2. **Memory safety**: `leaves_ptr` raw pointers are derived from a single `DeviceBuffer` that outlives all worker threads (scoped threads join before `leaves` is returned). No thread reads from another thread's write region.\n183\t\n184\t3. **GPU memory budget**: Each thread allocates its own `intermediates` (at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size * sizeof(EF)` ≈ few MB) and `tmp` (at most `height * num_interactions * sizeof(Frac<EF>)`). With 4 threads, peak additional GPU memory is ~4x one thread's allocation. This is bounded because the largest AIRs (which have the biggest buffers) are distributed across different threads by the height-descending sort.\n185\t\n186\t4. **APC 0 no-regression**: The 100-AIR threshold ensures APC 0 (99 AIR instances across 5 segments, ~20 per segment) uses the sequential path. This avoids the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task.\n187\t\n188\t5. **Determinism**: The `leaves` buffer content is deterministic regardless of thread scheduling because each AIR writes to a fixed, non-overlapping offset. The function returns the same `(leaves, alpha_logup)` as before.\n189\t\n190\t6. **Stream visibility**: `current_stream_sync()` before spawning threads ensures all prior GPU operations (trace uploads, pk data, `fill_zero()`) are visible to worker streams. After threads join, the main thread's `cudaStreamPerThread` will see all worker writes through CUDA's implicit synchronization at `std::thread::scope` join.\n191\t\n192\t## Measurement Plan\n193\t\n194\t### Before measurement\n195\tRun the prove command for APC {0, 100, 300} on the current code (already done — results in `measure_apc{000,100,300}/`).\n196\t\n197\t### After measurement\n198\tRun the same prove commands after implementing the changes:\n199\t\n200\t```bash\n201\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n202\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc000.json --recursion\n203\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc100.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc100.json --recursion\n204\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc300.json --recursion\n205\t```\n206\t\n207\tAnalyze with `spec.py` and compare:\n208\t\n209\t| Metric | Target |\n210\t|--------|--------|\n211\t| LogUp GKR (APC 300) | < 650ms (currently 808ms, >20% improvement) |\n212\t| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1830ms (currently 1986ms, >8% improvement) |\n213\t| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | No regression (within 3% noise, currently 2118ms) |\n214\t| Round 0 (APC 300) | No regression (currently 349ms) |\n215\t\n216\t### Profiling\n217\tRun nsight on APC 300 to verify concurrent kernel execution:\n218\t\n219\t```bash\n220\tnsys profile --output profile_after --trace cuda,nvtx --sample none -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n221\t```\n222\t\n223\tVerify that `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances show overlapping time ranges across different CUDA streams in the nsight timeline.\n224\t\n225\t## Rollback Criteria\n226\t\n227\tRevert if ANY of:\n228\t- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10% (< 80ms improvement from 808ms)\n229\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (> 2182ms)\n230\t- Any APC config fails prove+verify\n231\t- Peak GPU memory increases by more than 2GB at any APC config\n232\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ro6v4k6ZAeVEeuJs6zqc5v","session_id":"93785c5e-49ce-4ca5-bdce-28569746575a","uuid":"0f2285d8-fa96-4bff-870c-36645a55ed56","timestamp":"2026-04-12T23:14:55.336Z"}
