{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RJERG2Xfh7yvaYCiazEAfE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Multi-stream GKR Input Evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce LogUp GKR time at APC 300 by parallelizing the GKR input evaluation across multiple OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams. This targets the single largest component of STARK excl trace (808ms / 41%), specifically the `log_gkr_input_evals()` function which launches 791 sequential per-AIR kernel calls totaling 428ms of GPU time. With 4 concurrent streams, we expect to reduce input eval wall clock from ~450ms to ~180-220ms, yielding a ~200-250ms reduction in LogUp GKR and a ~10-13% improvement in STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:274` calls `log_gkr_input_evals()` at line 344.\n12\t\n13\t### Target function\n14\t\n15\t`log_gkr_input_evals()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:89-221`:\n16\t\n17\t1. **Line 102-103**: Allocates `leaves` buffer (`DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` with capacity `total_leaves`) and calls `fill_zero()`.\n18\t2. **Lines 106-107**: Creates shared mutable buffers `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` (reused across iterations).\n19\t3. **Lines 108-216**: Sequential `for meta in trace_interactions.iter().flatten()` loop. For each AIR with interactions:\n20\t   - **Lines 112-121**: Gathers preprocessed matrix and partitioned main matrices (read-only pointers).\n21\t   - **Lines 130-133**: H2D copy of `public_values` (small, per-AIR).\n22\t   - **Lines 138-145**: H2D copy of `partition_ptrs` into `d_partition_ptrs` (reused buffer, small).\n23\t   - **Lines 147-154**: Allocates `intermediates` buffer (per-iteration, freed at loop end).\n24\t   - **Lines 158-176**: Computes `dst_offset` into `leaves`, determines if lifting is needed, gets `trace_output` pointer (either `leaves_ptr` or `tmp`).\n25\t   - **Lines 177-191**: Launches `logup_gkr_input_eval` GPU kernel.\n26\t   - **Lines 193-214**: If lifting needed, launches `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` and `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` GPU kernels.\n27\t\n28\t### Why it is slow\n29\t\n30\tNsight profiling at APC 300 shows:\n31\t- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>`: 374.9ms across 486 instances (avg 771us each)\n32\t- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`: 53.6ms across 305 instances (avg 176us each)\n33\t- Total: 428.5ms GPU kernel time, ~450ms wall clock\n34\t\n35\tEach kernel uses 1-4 SMs on the RTX 4090's 128 SMs. Running sequentially on a single CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`), >95% of GPU compute capacity is idle during each kernel launch. The per-AIR operations are fully independent — they read from different trace partitions and write to non-overlapping offsets in the `leaves` buffer.\n36\t\n37\t### Successful precedent\n38\t\n39\tThe Round 0 multi-stream optimization (task `2026-04-13-0100-multistream-round0-parallel`) achieved 1.89x improvement at APC 300 using the same pattern: distributing independent per-AIR work across 4 OS threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread`. The kernel size distribution is similar (Round 0 avg ~600us, GKR input avg ~540us).\n40\t\n41\t## Changes\n42\t\n43\t### Change 1: Add `GkrInputWorkItem` struct\n44\t\n45\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n46\t\n47\tAdd a struct that holds all read-only references needed to process one AIR's GKR input evaluation:\n48\t\n49\t```rust\n50\tstruct GkrInputWorkItem<'a, HS: GpuHashScheme> {\n51\t    meta: &'a TraceInteractionMeta,\n52\t    air_ctx: &'a AirProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n53\t    pk_air: &'a DeviceStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n54\t    l_skip: usize,\n55\t    d_challenges: &'a DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n56\t    /// Raw pointer into the pre-allocated, zero-filled `leaves` buffer at this AIR's offset.\n57\t    leaves_ptr: *mut Frac<EF>,\n58\t}\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\t**Why**: Encapsulates per-AIR data so that the worker function can process one AIR independently, without mutable shared state. The `leaves_ptr` is a raw pointer because different AIRs write to non-overlapping regions of the same buffer; Rust's borrow checker cannot express this directly, but it is safe because the stacked layout guarantees non-overlapping offsets.\n62\t\n63\t`leaves_ptr` must be wrapped in a newtype that implements `Send`:\n64\t\n65\t```rust\n66\tstruct SendPtr<T>(*mut T);\n67\tunsafe impl<T> Send for SendPtr<T> {}\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t### Change 2: Add `process_gkr_input_air()` function\n71\t\n72\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n73\t\n74\tExtract the loop body (lines 109-215) into a standalone function:\n75\t\n76\t```rust\n77\tfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n78\t    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n79\t    d_partition_ptrs: &mut DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n80\t    tmp: &mut DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n81\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n82\t    let null_preprocessed = DeviceBuffer::<F>::new();\n83\t    // ... same logic as the current loop body, but using w.leaves_ptr.0\n84\t    // instead of indexing into a shared leaves buffer.\n85\t    //\n86\t    // d_partition_ptrs and tmp are passed in as grow-only buffers per thread\n87\t    // (matching the current sequential code's reuse pattern at lines 106-107, 142-144, 170-172).\n88\t    //\n89\t    // Each call creates its own:\n90\t    //   - intermediates (GPU buffer, per-iteration as in current code)\n91\t    //   - d_public_values (H2D copy, small)\n92\t    //   - null_preprocessed (zero-cost, just a null DeviceBuffer)\n93\t}\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\t**Why**: A standalone function can be called from any thread. Each thread will get its own `cudaStreamPerThread`, so GPU kernel launches from different threads execute on different CUDA streams concurrently.\n97\t\n98\tThe `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers follow the current code's grow-only pattern (lines 106-107, 142-144, 170-172): allocated once per thread, grown as needed within the thread's chunk. This avoids repeated malloc/free cycles and reduces `MemoryManager` mutex contention compared to per-AIR allocation.\n99\t\n100\tThe `null_preprocessed` is created per call as `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer, zero cost — no GPU allocation). The `intermediates` buffer is allocated per-AIR as in the current code (freed at function return). The `d_public_values` H2D copy is small (a few field elements).\n101\t\n102\tPer-thread peak memory is bounded: `d_partition_ptrs` is tiny (2-3 pointers = 16-24 bytes), `intermediates` capacity is at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` (typically a few MB), and `tmp` is `height * num_interactions` (varies but bounded by the largest AIR in the chunk).\n103\t\n104\t### Change 3: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals()` into 3 phases\n105\t\n106\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n107\t\n108\tReplace the sequential loop with a three-phase structure:\n109\t\n110\t**Phase 1 (CPU, main thread)**: Build work items and sort by descending height for load balance.\n111\t\n112\t```rust\n113\tlet mut work_items: Vec<GkrInputWorkItem<HS>> = trace_interactions\n114\t    .iter()\n115\t    .flatten()\n116\t    .map(|meta| {\n117\t        let air_ctx = &ctx.per_trace[meta.trace_idx].1;\n118\t        let pk_air = &pk.per_air[meta.air_idx];\n119\t        let slice = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap();\n120\t        assert_eq!(slice.col_idx, 0);\n121\t        let dst_offset = slice.row_idx;\n122\t        let leaves_ptr = unsafe { leaves.as_mut_ptr().add(dst_offset) };\n123\t        GkrInputWorkItem {\n124\t            meta,\n125\t            air_ctx,\n126\t            pk_air,\n127\t            l_skip,\n128\t            d_challenges,\n129\t            leaves_ptr: SendPtr(leaves_ptr),\n130\t        }\n131\t    })\n132\t    .collect();\n133\t\n134\twork_items.sort_by(|a, b| b.air_ctx.height().cmp(&a.air_ctx.height()));\n135\t```\n136\t\n137\t**Sync barrier**: Call `current_stream_sync()` to ensure the `leaves.fill_zero()` and any prior GPU work (trace uploads, pk data) is visible to worker thread streams.\n138\t\n139\t**Phase 2 (GPU, multi-threaded)**: Process AIRs in parallel.\n140\t\n141\t```rust\n142\tconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;\n143\t\n144\tlet num_threads = if work_items.len() >= 100 {\n145\t    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(work_items.len())\n146\t} else {\n147\t    1\n148\t};\n149\t\n150\tif num_threads <= 1 {\n151\t    let mut d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::<u64>::new();\n152\t    let mut tmp = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::new();\n153\t    for w in &work_items {\n154\t        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut d_partition_ptrs, &mut tmp)?;\n155\t    }\n156\t} else {\n157\t    let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n158\t    std::thread::scope(|s| {\n159\t        let handles: Vec<_> = work_items\n160\t            .chunks(chunk_size)\n161\t            .map(|chunk| {\n162\t                s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n163\t                    let mut d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::<u64>::new();\n164\t                    let mut tmp = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::new();\n165\t                    for w in chunk {\n166\t                        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut d_partition_ptrs, &mut tmp)?;\n167\t                    }\n168\t                    // Explicit stream sync to ensure all GPU writes from this\n169\t                    // thread's stream are globally visible before thread exit.\n170\t                    // This makes the synchronization invariant explicit rather\n171\t                    // than relying on the CudaThreadCleanup TLS destructor.\n172\t                    current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n173\t                    Ok(())\n174\t                })\n175\t            })\n176\t            .collect();\n177\t        for handle in handles {\n178\t            handle.join().unwrap()?;\n179\t        }\n180\t        Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n181\t    })?;\n182\t}\n183\t```\n184\t\n185\t**Why**: Same approach as the successful Round 0 multi-stream. `std::thread::scope` with N threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread` for concurrent kernel execution. The threshold of 100 applies to `work_items.len()`, which is the count of AIRs-with-interactions (the flatten count from `trace_interactions`). At APC 0 (99 total AIR instances across 5 segments, ~20 per segment), the flatten count will be at most 99 (and likely less since some AIRs may lack interactions), so APC 0 uses the sequential path. This avoids the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task. At APC 100 (~360 AIR instances) and APC 300 (~623 AIR instances), the flatten count is well above 100.\n186\t\n187\t**Phase 3**: None needed. Unlike Round 0, the results are written directly into the `leaves` buffer via raw pointers during Phase 2 — there is no CPU-side scatter step.\n188\t\n189\t### Change 4: Reuse `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` constant or define `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS`\n190\t\n191\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n192\t\n193\tDefine a constant `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;` for the number of worker threads. This matches `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` in `mod.rs:93` and can be tuned independently if needed.\n194\t\n195\t**Why**: Keeps the GKR input parallelism configuration independent from Round 0, in case they need different tuning (e.g., if GKR input has different memory pressure characteristics).\n196\t\n197\t## Invariants\n198\t\n199\t1. **Correctness**: Each AIR writes to a non-overlapping region of `leaves`, determined by `dst_offset = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap().row_idx`. The stacked layout guarantees these regions do not overlap. The `fill_zero()` call happens before the sync barrier, ensuring all regions start zeroed.\n200\t\n201\t2. **Memory safety**: `leaves_ptr` raw pointers are derived from a single `DeviceBuffer` that outlives all worker threads (scoped threads join before `leaves` is returned). No thread reads from another thread's write region.\n202\t\n203\t3. **GPU memory budget**: Each thread allocates its own `intermediates` (at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size * sizeof(EF)` ≈ few MB) and `tmp` (at most `height * num_interactions * sizeof(Frac<EF>)`). With 4 threads, peak additional GPU memory is ~4x one thread's allocation. This is bounded because the largest AIRs (which have the biggest buffers) are distributed across different threads by the height-descending sort.\n204\t\n205\t4. **APC 0 no-regression**: The 100-AIR threshold ensures APC 0 (99 AIR instances across 5 segments, ~20 per segment) uses the sequential path. This avoids the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task.\n206\t\n207\t5. **Determinism**: The `leaves` buffer content is deterministic regardless of thread scheduling because each AIR writes to a fixed, non-overlapping offset. The function returns the same `(leaves, alpha_logup)` as before.\n208\t\n209\t6. **Stream visibility**: `current_stream_sync()` before spawning threads ensures all prior GPU operations (trace uploads, pk data, `fill_zero()`) are visible to worker streams. Each worker thread calls `current_stream_sync()` as its last operation, which calls `cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread)` to drain the worker's CUDA stream and ensure all GPU writes are globally visible. `std::thread::scope` guarantees all spawned threads (including their `current_stream_sync()` calls) complete before returning, so the main thread sees all worker GPU writes. This explicit sync avoids depending on the `CudaThreadCleanup` TLS destructor (`crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs:101-108`), making the correctness invariant visible in the code.\n210\t\n211\t## Measurement Plan\n212\t\n213\t### Before measurement\n214\tRun the prove command for APC {0, 100, 300} on the current code (already done — results in `measure_apc{000,100,300}/`).\n215\t\n216\t### After measurement\n217\tRun the same prove commands after implementing the changes:\n218\t\n219\t```bash\n220\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n221\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc000.json --recursion\n222\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc100.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc100.json --recursion\n223\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc300.json --recursion\n224\t```\n225\t\n226\tAnalyze with `spec.py` and compare:\n227\t\n228\t| Metric | Target |\n229\t|--------|--------|\n230\t| LogUp GKR (APC 300) | < 650ms (currently 808ms, >20% improvement) |\n231\t| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1830ms (currently 1986ms, >8% improvement) |\n232\t| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | No regression (within 3% noise, currently 2118ms) |\n233\t| Round 0 (APC 300) | No regression (currently 349ms) |\n234\t\n235\t### Profiling\n236\tRun nsight on APC 300 to verify concurrent kernel execution:\n237\t\n238\t```bash\n239\tnsys profile --output profile_after --trace cuda,nvtx --sample none -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\tVerify that `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances show overlapping time ranges across different CUDA streams in the nsight timeline.\n243\t\n244\t## Rollback Criteria\n245\t\n246\tRevert if ANY of:\n247\t- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10% (< 80ms improvement from 808ms)\n248\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (> 2182ms)\n249\t- Any APC config fails prove+verify\n250\t- Peak GPU memory increases by more than 2GB at any APC config\n251\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93785c5e-49ce-4ca5-bdce-28569746575a","uuid":"7beb8864-3ab0-4ddd-a7bb-dd86b07231cf","timestamp":"2026-04-12T23:22:05.577Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-0115-multistream-gkr-input-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Multi-stream GKR Input Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR time at APC 300 by parallelizing the GKR input evaluation across multiple OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams. This targets the single largest component of STARK excl trace (808ms / 41%), specifically the `log_gkr_input_evals()` function which launches 791 sequential per-AIR kernel calls totaling 428ms of GPU time. With 4 concurrent streams, we expect to reduce input eval wall clock from ~450ms to ~180-220ms, yielding a ~200-250ms reduction in LogUp GKR and a ~10-13% improvement in STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n\n`prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:274` calls `log_gkr_input_evals()` at line 344.\n\n### Target function\n\n`log_gkr_input_evals()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:89-221`:\n\n1. **Line 102-103**: Allocates `leaves` buffer (`DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` with capacity `total_leaves`) and calls `fill_zero()`.\n2. **Lines 106-107**: Creates shared mutable buffers `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` (reused across iterations).\n3. **Lines 108-216**: Sequential `for meta in trace_interactions.iter().flatten()` loop. For each AIR with interactions:\n   - **Lines 112-121**: Gathers preprocessed matrix and partitioned main matrices (read-only pointers).\n   - **Lines 130-133**: H2D copy of `public_values` (small, per-AIR).\n   - **Lines 138-145**: H2D copy of `partition_ptrs` into `d_partition_ptrs` (reused buffer, small).\n   - **Lines 147-154**: Allocates `intermediates` buffer (per-iteration, freed at loop end).\n   - **Lines 158-176**: Computes `dst_offset` into `leaves`, determines if lifting is needed, gets `trace_output` pointer (either `leaves_ptr` or `tmp`).\n   - **Lines 177-191**: Launches `logup_gkr_input_eval` GPU kernel.\n   - **Lines 193-214**: If lifting needed, launches `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` and `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` GPU kernels.\n\n### Why it is slow\n\nNsight profiling at APC 300 shows:\n- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>`: 374.9ms across 486 instances (avg 771us each)\n- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`: 53.6ms across 305 instances (avg 176us each)\n- Total: 428.5ms GPU kernel time, ~450ms wall clock\n\nEach kernel uses 1-4 SMs on the RTX 4090's 128 SMs. Running sequentially on a single CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`), >95% of GPU compute capacity is idle during each kernel launch. The per-AIR operations are fully independent — they read from different trace partitions and write to non-overlapping offsets in the `leaves` buffer.\n\n### Successful precedent\n\nThe Round 0 multi-stream optimization (task `2026-04-13-0100-multistream-round0-parallel`) achieved 1.89x improvement at APC 300 using the same pattern: distributing independent per-AIR work across 4 OS threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread`. The kernel size distribution is similar (Round 0 avg ~600us, GKR input avg ~540us).\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `GkrInputWorkItem` struct\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nAdd a struct that holds all read-only references needed to process one AIR's GKR input evaluation:\n\n```rust\nstruct GkrInputWorkItem<'a, HS: GpuHashScheme> {\n    meta: &'a TraceInteractionMeta,\n    air_ctx: &'a AirProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    pk_air: &'a DeviceStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    l_skip: usize,\n    d_challenges: &'a DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    /// Raw pointer into the pre-allocated, zero-filled `leaves` buffer at this AIR's offset.\n    leaves_ptr: *mut Frac<EF>,\n}\n```\n\n**Why**: Encapsulates per-AIR data so that the worker function can process one AIR independently, without mutable shared state. The `leaves_ptr` is a raw pointer because different AIRs write to non-overlapping regions of the same buffer; Rust's borrow checker cannot express this directly, but it is safe because the stacked layout guarantees non-overlapping offsets.\n\n`leaves_ptr` must be wrapped in a newtype that implements `Send`:\n\n```rust\nstruct SendPtr<T>(*mut T);\nunsafe impl<T> Send for SendPtr<T> {}\n```\n\n### Change 2: Add `process_gkr_input_air()` function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nExtract the loop body (lines 109-215) into a standalone function:\n\n```rust\nfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n    d_partition_ptrs: &mut DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n    tmp: &mut DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n    let null_preprocessed = DeviceBuffer::<F>::new();\n    // ... same logic as the current loop body, but using w.leaves_ptr.0\n    // instead of indexing into a shared leaves buffer.\n    //\n    // d_partition_ptrs and tmp are passed in as grow-only buffers per thread\n    // (matching the current sequential code's reuse pattern at lines 106-107, 142-144, 170-172).\n    //\n    // Each call creates its own:\n    //   - intermediates (GPU buffer, per-iteration as in current code)\n    //   - d_public_values (H2D copy, small)\n    //   - null_preprocessed (zero-cost, just a null DeviceBuffer)\n}\n```\n\n**Why**: A standalone function can be called from any thread. Each thread will get its own `cudaStreamPerThread`, so GPU kernel launches from different threads execute on different CUDA streams concurrently.\n\nThe `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers follow the current code's grow-only pattern (lines 106-107, 142-144, 170-172): allocated once per thread, grown as needed within the thread's chunk. This avoids repeated malloc/free cycles and reduces `MemoryManager` mutex contention compared to per-AIR allocation.\n\nThe `null_preprocessed` is created per call as `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer, zero cost — no GPU allocation). The `intermediates` buffer is allocated per-AIR as in the current code (freed at function return). The `d_public_values` H2D copy is small (a few field elements).\n\nPer-thread peak memory is bounded: `d_partition_ptrs` is tiny (2-3 pointers = 16-24 bytes), `intermediates` capacity is at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` (typically a few MB), and `tmp` is `height * num_interactions` (varies but bounded by the largest AIR in the chunk).\n\n### Change 3: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals()` into 3 phases\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nReplace the sequential loop with a three-phase structure:\n\n**Phase 1 (CPU, main thread)**: Build work items and sort by descending height for load balance.\n\n```rust\nlet mut work_items: Vec<GkrInputWorkItem<HS>> = trace_interactions\n    .iter()\n    .flatten()\n    .map(|meta| {\n        let air_ctx = &ctx.per_trace[meta.trace_idx].1;\n        let pk_air = &pk.per_air[meta.air_idx];\n        let slice = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap();\n        assert_eq!(slice.col_idx, 0);\n        let dst_offset = slice.row_idx;\n        let leaves_ptr = unsafe { leaves.as_mut_ptr().add(dst_offset) };\n        GkrInputWorkItem {\n            meta,\n            air_ctx,\n            pk_air,\n            l_skip,\n            d_challenges,\n            leaves_ptr: SendPtr(leaves_ptr),\n        }\n    })\n    .collect();\n\nwork_items.sort_by(|a, b| b.air_ctx.height().cmp(&a.air_ctx.height()));\n```\n\n**Sync barrier**: Call `current_stream_sync()` to ensure the `leaves.fill_zero()` and any prior GPU work (trace uploads, pk data) is visible to worker thread streams.\n\n**Phase 2 (GPU, multi-threaded)**: Process AIRs in parallel.\n\n```rust\nconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;\n\nlet num_threads = if work_items.len() >= 100 {\n    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(work_items.len())\n} else {\n    1\n};\n\nif num_threads <= 1 {\n    let mut d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::<u64>::new();\n    let mut tmp = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::new();\n    for w in &work_items {\n        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut d_partition_ptrs, &mut tmp)?;\n    }\n} else {\n    let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n    std::thread::scope(|s| {\n        let handles: Vec<_> = work_items\n            .chunks(chunk_size)\n            .map(|chunk| {\n                s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                    let mut d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::<u64>::new();\n                    let mut tmp = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::new();\n                    for w in chunk {\n                        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut d_partition_ptrs, &mut tmp)?;\n                    }\n                    // Explicit stream sync to ensure all GPU writes from this\n                    // thread's stream are globally visible before thread exit.\n                    // This makes the synchronization invariant explicit rather\n                    // than relying on the CudaThreadCleanup TLS destructor.\n                    current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                    Ok(())\n                })\n            })\n            .collect();\n        for handle in handles {\n            handle.join().unwrap()?;\n        }\n        Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n    })?;\n}\n```\n\n**Why**: Same approach as the successful Round 0 multi-stream. `std::thread::scope` with N threads, each with `cudaStreamPerThread` for concurrent kernel execution. The threshold of 100 applies to `work_items.len()`, which is the count of AIRs-with-interactions (the flatten count from `trace_interactions`). At APC 0 (99 total AIR instances across 5 segments, ~20 per segment), the flatten count will be at most 99 (and likely less since some AIRs may lack interactions), so APC 0 uses the sequential path. This avoids the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task. At APC 100 (~360 AIR instances) and APC 300 (~623 AIR instances), the flatten count is well above 100.\n\n**Phase 3**: None needed. Unlike Round 0, the results are written directly into the `leaves` buffer via raw pointers during Phase 2 — there is no CPU-side scatter step.\n\n### Change 4: Reuse `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` constant or define `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nDefine a constant `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;` for the number of worker threads. This matches `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` in `mod.rs:93` and can be tuned independently if needed.\n\n**Why**: Keeps the GKR input parallelism configuration independent from Round 0, in case they need different tuning (e.g., if GKR input has different memory pressure characteristics).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Each AIR writes to a non-overlapping region of `leaves`, determined by `dst_offset = meta.layout_slices.first().unwrap().row_idx`. The stacked layout guarantees these regions do not overlap. The `fill_zero()` call happens before the sync barrier, ensuring all regions start zeroed.\n\n2. **Memory safety**: `leaves_ptr` raw pointers are derived from a single `DeviceBuffer` that outlives all worker threads (scoped threads join before `leaves` is returned). No thread reads from another thread's write region.\n\n3. **GPU memory budget**: Each thread allocates its own `intermediates` (at most `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size * sizeof(EF)` ≈ few MB) and `tmp` (at most `height * num_interactions * sizeof(Frac<EF>)`). With 4 threads, peak additional GPU memory is ~4x one thread's allocation. This is bounded because the largest AIRs (which have the biggest buffers) are distributed across different threads by the height-descending sort.\n\n4. **APC 0 no-regression**: The 100-AIR threshold ensures APC 0 (99 AIR instances across 5 segments, ~20 per segment) uses the sequential path. This avoids the memory pool contention issue observed in the Round 0 task.\n\n5. **Determinism**: The `leaves` buffer content is deterministic regardless of thread scheduling because each AIR writes to a fixed, non-overlapping offset. The function returns the same `(leaves, alpha_logup)` as before.\n\n6. **Stream visibility**: `current_stream_sync()` before spawning threads ensures all prior GPU operations (trace uploads, pk data, `fill_zero()`) are visible to worker streams. Each worker thread calls `current_stream_sync()` as its last operation, which calls `cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread)` to drain the worker's CUDA stream and ensure all GPU writes are globally visible. `std::thread::scope` guarantees all spawned threads (including their `current_stream_sync()` calls) complete before returning, so the main thread sees all worker GPU writes. This explicit sync avoids depending on the `CudaThreadCleanup` TLS destructor (`crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs:101-108`), making the correctness invariant visible in the code.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Before measurement\nRun the prove command for APC {0, 100, 300} on the current code (already done — results in `measure_apc{000,100,300}/`).\n\n### After measurement\nRun the same prove commands after implementing the changes:\n\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc000.json --recursion\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc100.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc100.json --recursion\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc300.json --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with `spec.py` and compare:\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| LogUp GKR (APC 300) | < 650ms (currently 808ms, >20% improvement) |\n| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1830ms (currently 1986ms, >8% improvement) |\n| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | No regression (within 3% noise, currently 2118ms) |\n| Round 0 (APC 300) | No regression (currently 349ms) |\n\n### Profiling\nRun nsight on APC 300 to verify concurrent kernel execution:\n\n```bash\nnsys profile --output profile_after --trace cuda,nvtx --sample none -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n```\n\nVerify that `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances show overlapping time ranges across different CUDA streams in the nsight timeline.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10% (< 80ms improvement from 808ms)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (> 2182ms)\n- Any APC config fails prove+verify\n- Peak GPU memory increases by more than 2GB at any APC config\n","numLines":251,"startLine":1,"totalLines":251}}}
